dence-protocol\",\"requirement\":\"Provider-wide node-restart evidence protocol on node1 and node2 (independent of public task contract)\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"Both nodes show a full transition: pre-restart healthy baseline (boot_id, CA/cert files, trust anchor, nginx active, HTTPS 200) via cmd-1f0ef4ed71c448c4/cmd-25e17ccab3c94fea/cmd-4bbc2d201eb94bb5, systemctl reboot acknowledgement with unreachability during the transition, a differing post-reboot boot_id (node1: fb2aa416→200e77fb; node2: bccb87ac→7052aa67) confirmed both by executor commands and independently by the fixed after-executor lifecycle snapshot, and recovered state (CA/trust intact on node1, nginx auto-started with cert/firewall/trust intact on node2, end-to-end HTTPS 200 in both directions post-reboot).\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-1f0ef4ed71c448c4\",\"cmd-25e17ccab3c94fea\",\"cmd-21b35406dd7e4ec7\",\"cmd-c95e0123232d4041\",\"cmd-c344c5345f074252\",\"cmd-4d5f134b4b6f4e0f\",\"cmd-50d4d09bb7fa4e4a\",\"cmd-d3d111e6b62e442d\",\"cmd-5edaf672131140d3\",\"global:after_executor:node1:boot-identity\",\"global:after_executor:node2:boot-identity\"]}],\"confidence\":0.95,\"operational_hygiene\":{\"score\":0.9,\"summary\":\"Command trace shows a clean progression with only minor, low-risk noise: (1) cmd-a951688060704987 was a failed CA self-sign attempt (bad `-config /dev/null` with v3_ca extension) that produced private/ca.key.pem, which was then reused (not regenerated) by the corrected, successful cmd-25f84fc95851458b — a bounded, non-destructive retry directly leading to the final CA, not a wasted mutation. (2) cmd-d001d66f80c94aa2 was a failed SSH auth attempt (permission denied) that was a necessary discovery step before correctly provisioning node2's authorized_keys in cmd-0f616da3f8b043bb, a required prerequisite for the scp/ssh deployment steps that followed. All other state-changing commands (CA directory setup, cert/key generation, nginx install/config, firewalld port opening, trust anchor installation) map directly to material task outcomes. Minor residue: nginx's default install left /tmp/nginx.pid and /tmp/nginx_install.log on node2, and the default nginx vhost on port 80 remains listening (stock nginx package behavior, not explicitly configured or insecure) — both are low-risk incidental artifacts of the required nginx installation rather than unnecessary executor mutations.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-a951688060704987\",\"cmd-25f84fc95851458b\",\"cmd-d001d66f80c94aa2\",\"cmd-0f616da3f8b043bb\",\"global:after_executor:node2:temporary-files\",\"global:after_executor:node2:listening-sockets\"]},\"overall_summary\":\"All four material outcomes of the task contract are directly satisfied with coherent evidence: a self-signed 'Fleet Internal CA' was created on node1, a CA-signed TLS certificate with the correct SAN was issued to node2, node2 serves HTTPS on 8443 using that certificate (confirmed by both curl tests and the independent lifecycle socket snapshot), and the CA was installed as a trusted system anchor on both nodes, verified by unqualified (no --cacert) curl succeeding via system trust on both. The separate provider-wide restart protocol was also executed correctly for both nodes, with pre-restart baseline, acknowledged reboot, changed boot identity, and full post-reboot recovery all directly observed and cross-checked against the fixed lifecycle snapshots.\",\"limitations\":[\"No independent (non-executor) evidence beyond the fixed lifecycle snapshots was available to corroborate command outputs; all functional verification relies on executor-run curl/trust/openssl commands.\",\"The lifecycle 'after_prepare' phase is aliased to 'before_prepare' since prepare was disabled, so preparer-vs-executor state separation relies entirely on the before_prepare/after_executor diff.\"]}","structured_output":{"requirements":[{"id":"ca-created","requirement":"Create a certificate authority named 'Fleet Internal CA' on node1","status":"satisfied","summar